{"title":"2025 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 9","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(Rectangular_Form)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (Rectangular Form)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Polar_Form)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Polar Form)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Coordinate_Conversion,_Similar_to_Solution_1)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Coordinate Conversion, Similar to Solution 1)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_(Quick_and_Intuitive!)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution (Quick and Intuitive!)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Power_Solve\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Power Solve</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4b825a2e36ae093ed7be5e4851ef453b34914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> be the complex number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/8/428c37d79dd642411dd1522d419c81af0a1dc2ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2+i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"39\" height=\"15\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/9/75952e52a3dc061d24146c0df9062a54a5ce5a5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i=\\sqrt{-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"71\" height=\"21\" />. What real number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> has the property that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4b825a2e36ae093ed7be5e4851ef453b34914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/4/8d4e1e46efbf077b2fa276dad459a6f0840a4dc2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" /> are three collinear points in the complex plane?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/1/431d9e4c5417dbbf08ec45ba26d8279c1fb17395.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}~\\frac34\\qquad\\textbf{(B)}~1\\qquad\\textbf{(C)}~\\frac75\\qquad\\textbf{(D)}~\\frac32\\qquad\\textbf{(E)}~\\frac53$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"367\" height=\"38\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28Rectangular_Form.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(Rectangular_Form)\">Solution 1 (Rectangular Form)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (Rectangular Form)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We begin by calculating <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/4/8d4e1e46efbf077b2fa276dad459a6f0840a4dc2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/e/d6e245ffba1ed063eeee0ef1546add4edd1fdfb1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[w^2 = (2+i)^2 = 4+4i-1 = 3+4i.\\]\" width=\"281\" height=\"20\" />\nValues on the complex plane can easily be represented as points on the Cartesian plane, so we go ahead and do that so we are in a more familiar place. \nTranslating onto the Cartesian plane, we have the points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/6/aa6bb1c456d71ee24e7a0ee603d226a7992e5da3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2,1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/f/97fadfe6bdbd1adb164f263b52f2a290723ad45c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(3,4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" />. The slope of the line passing through these points is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/7/b07e751c0f9bd286ce502487efa40d7ace7c5d63.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4-1}{3-2} = 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" />, so the equation of this line is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/e/e4e853be2df625626b2314767e873aa7ba23960c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} y &amp;= 3(x-2)+1 \\\\ y &amp;= 3x-5. \\end{align*}\" width=\"128\" height=\"42\" />\nWe want the real number that passes through this line, which is equivalent to the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/5/31572a833f4041c91695248e85c6f3a17ed948fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x-$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"26\" height=\"14\" />intercept. This occurs when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/8/ec89f09c84538ec43930ca5d8e5e0957e2000822.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"45\" height=\"17\" />, so the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-intercept of this line is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/2/d82e82be03299987d7e166c438c921097458dd09.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\boxed{\\textbf{(E)}~\\frac53}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"99\" height=\"49\" />\n</p><p>~lprado (minor edits ~Logibyte)\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Polar_Form.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Polar_Form)\">Solution 2 (Polar Form)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Polar Form)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Recall that the slope of a line is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/3/8f3ffa28ff4c456bf3d61f2a1ffb2f23124ee3ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=\\tan\\phi,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"87\" height=\"18\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/1/2c175f60eecef1de7560c3bdea495d69f26f719d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is the angle formed by the line and the positive <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-axis.\n</p><p>Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/2/5b2f375fb621fb31bc8f09800852403cfe44a67a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|w|=\\sqrt{2^2+1^2}=\\sqrt{5}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"174\" height=\"24\" /> In polar coordinates, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/3/4437b12fe65abc5fe9965cacd09800f31ba54da3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cis}\\theta.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"104\" height=\"21\" /> It follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/60159e9595a30344d3a79a7ce14f89cfd299705c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tan\\theta=\\frac12.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"83\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>By De Moivre's Theorem, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/6/2d612eafe7110f41af804bb03ef6b07b64c5bf2c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w^2=5\\operatorname{cis}(2\\theta),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"117\" height=\"21\" /> from which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/0/b6020c0c7ce0c12cbe39ac76fb7886e5d6f9d2a7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\tan(2\\theta)=\\frac{2\\tan\\theta}{1-\\tan^2\\theta}=\\frac{4}{3}.\\]\" width=\"204\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>We obtain the following diagram:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/b/7cbc0b2296f36d19eb18f265a417d6504f2390e6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] /* Made by MRENTHUSIASM */ size(200);   int xMin = -2; int xMax = 5; int yMin = -2; int yMax = 5;  //Draws the horizontal gridlines void horizontalLines() {   for (int i = yMin+1; i &lt; yMax; ++i)   {     draw((xMin,i)--(xMax,i), mediumgray+linewidth(0.4));   } }  //Draws the vertical gridlines void verticalLines() {   for (int i = xMin+1; i &lt; xMax; ++i)   {     draw((i,yMin)--(i,yMax), mediumgray+linewidth(0.4));   } }  horizontalLines(); verticalLines(); draw((xMin,0)--(xMax,0),black+linewidth(1.5),EndArrow(5)); draw((0,yMin)--(0,yMax),black+linewidth(1.5),EndArrow(5)); label(&quot;Re&quot;,(xMax,0),(2,0)); label(&quot;Im&quot;,(0,yMax),(0,2));  pair W1, W2, R;  W1 = (2,1); W2 = (3,4); R = (5/3,0);  label(&quot;$w=2+i$&quot;, W1, 2*dir(W1)); label(&quot;$w^2$&quot;, W2, 2*dir(W2)); label(&quot;$r$&quot;, R, 2*dir(R-W2)); label(&quot;$\\theta$&quot;, origin, 6*dir(14)); label(&quot;$\\theta$&quot;, origin, 6*dir(40));  draw(W1--origin--W2); draw(W2--R,dashed);  dot(W1^^W2^^R, linewidth(4)); [/asy]\" width=\"335\" height=\"325\" />\nSince <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/e/9ae0a43f802fd62c1ca95a8c4c48846a482cfd2a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left|w^2\\right|=5,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"73\" height=\"23\" /> we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/7/ba7e88fe953c73bff705118d1a32e7b4834a934f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w^2=3+4i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"94\" height=\"18\" /> by a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />-<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />-<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> triangle. The complex numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4b825a2e36ae093ed7be5e4851ef453b34914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/4/8d4e1e46efbf077b2fa276dad459a6f0840a4dc2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"23\" height=\"17\" /> correspond to the points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/6/aa6bb1c456d71ee24e7a0ee603d226a7992e5da3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2,1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/b/e6bc0d0817db5262d1af3b5fc5446d82dc6d4f79.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(3,4),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"47\" height=\"20\" /> respectively, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> corresponds to the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-intercept of this line. In slope-intercept form, the line containing these two points is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/1/4a10dbe8ab17328673c79f2798659808649c6c50.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=3x-5.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"91\" height=\"17\" /> Therefore, the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-intercept is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/0/980be530c661b8672c1bde27d5e1212d4040fe6c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r=\\boxed{\\textbf{(E)}~\\frac53}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"97\" height=\"49\" />\n</p><p>~MRENTHUSIASM\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Coordinate_Conversion.2C_Similar_to_Solution_1.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Coordinate_Conversion,_Similar_to_Solution_1)\">Solution 3 (Coordinate Conversion, Similar to Solution 1)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Coordinate Conversion, Similar to Solution 1)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Determine <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/a/52a0dd771645afff6940283c43c26f52d864e917.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2+i = (2,1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"104\" height=\"20\" /> on the real cartesian plane and notice <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/c/9ecd0bc73e582fd9aad1faee66e053df23659c45.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2+i)^2 = 3+4i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"132\" height=\"21\" /> takes the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/f/97fadfe6bdbd1adb164f263b52f2a290723ad45c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(3,4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" />. The slope of the line through the two points is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/9/4593fe36e0524804f8826eb733898b99781cb53f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3x-5 = y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"86\" height=\"17\" /> so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/0/7a06334068417b1781d14e8b28af827907961eb3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"42\" height=\"16\" /> gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/4/50455168639ceb9febd1bb513cbf240581c31db7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\boxed{\\frac{5}{3}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"61\" height=\"49\" />. Because the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> axis and the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/9/b997fd37cd7877511801547e80ebbf1f1542e0c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Re(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"39\" height=\"20\" /> axis are identical, we are finished.\n</p><p>~Pinotation, minor edit by KMSONI\n</p><p>The solution is here to show a different point of logic for the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> axis.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_.28Quick_and_Intuitive.21.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_(Quick_and_Intuitive!)\">Video Solution (Quick and Intuitive!)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution (Quick and Intuitive!)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/k-HoA8XEHl4\">https://youtu.be/k-HoA8XEHl4</a>\n</p><p>~Education, the Study of Everything\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/eu546TpNopc?si=nOAr_bymtH-Hwzqv\">https://youtu.be/eu546TpNopc?si=nOAr_bymtH-Hwzqv</a> - Continuum Math\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Power_Solve\">Video Solution by Power Solve</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Power Solve\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Oy5hEE1fmJU\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Oy5hEE1fmJU</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_SpreadTheMathLove\">Video Solution by SpreadTheMathLove</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by SpreadTheMathLove\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dAeyV60Hu5c\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dAeyV60Hu5c</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A\" title=\"2025 AMC 12A\">2025 AMC 12A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems\" title=\"2025 AMC 12A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2025 AMC 12A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"2025 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 8\">Problem 8</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2